{{also|Abrasion|abrasión|abrașiôn}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{etydate|1656}}. From {{bor|en|fr|abrasion}} (attested since 1611), from {{der|en|ML.|abrasio||a scraping}}, from {{der|en|la|abrādō||scrape off}}. See also ''[[abrade#Etymology|abrade]]''.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/əˈbɹeɪ.ʒn̩/|a=US}}
* {{audio|en|en-us-abrasion.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪʒən|s=3}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The act of [[abrading]], wearing, or rubbing off; the wearing away by friction. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 17<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED>{{R:SOED5|page=7}}</ref>
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The substance thus rubbed off; [[debris]]. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 18<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{senseid|en|Q188842}} {{lb|en|geology}} The effect of mechanical [[erosion]] of rock, especially a river bed, by rock fragments scratching and scraping it. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 19<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# An abraded, scraped, or worn area. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 20<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{senseid|en|superficial wound caused by scraping}} {{lb|en|medicine}} A [[superficial]] [[wound]] caused by scraping; an area of skin where the cells on the surface have been scraped or worn away. {{defdate|First attested in the mid 20<sup>th</sup> century.}}<ref name=SOED/>
# {{lb|en|dentistry}} The wearing away of the surface of the tooth by chewing.

